{"verdict":"changes_requested","issues":[{"severity":"blocker","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":148,"description":"The new app-level ContentstackContext.Provider is shadowed by the existing per-page providers, so draft content can never reach any consumer. The actual page content is rendered under pages/[[...slug]].tsx line 39 (`<ContentstackContext.Provider value={content}>`) and pages/404.tsx line 31, both of which set the context from static server-fetched props. Every real consumer (Homepage, Header, Layout, and the 147+ useContent consumers) reads from THAT inner provider, not the new one in _app.tsx. When onEntryChange fires, setPage updates only the outer app-level context value — the inner provider's `value={content}` is unchanged, so nothing the user sees re-renders with draft data. This fails every verification criterion that requires the rendered page to show the draft entry. Additionally, `pageProps.contentstackContext` (used to seed the state at line 73) is never produced by any getStaticProps — the slug page returns `content` and `contentTypeUid` only — so the app-level context starts as `{}` in production. The prescribed fix required the provider that actually feeds useContent consumers to carry the reactive state; this implements a parallel provider that feeds nobody.","suggestedFix":"Make the provider of record reactive: in pages/[[...slug]].tsx (the component that owns `<ContentstackContext.Provider value={content}>`), hold `page` in state seeded from `content.page` and update it from the onEntryChange callback, so the provider value that Homepage and all useContent consumers read is the one that changes. The setPage/onEntryChange wiring can stay in _app.tsx only if the updated entry is passed down so it overrides the page-level provider value (e.g., via a page-props-independent channel the slug page consumes). Verify end-to-end that after onEntryChange, the value read by useContent inside the rendered page reflects the draft entry."},{"severity":"blocker","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":109,"description":"The onEntryChange re-fetch never executes for the pages in scope. The guard `if (!contentTypeUid || !entryUid) return;` destructures both values from pageProps, but pages/[[...slug]].tsx getStaticProps returns only `content`, `contentTypeUid`, and translations — there is no `entryUid` prop. For the MX Homepage and Discover Article pages (both served through the catch-all slug route), `entryUid` is always undefined, the callback exits silently, and no draft re-fetch is ever issued even when a live preview hash is active. Combined with the shadowed-provider issue, the feature is doubly inert on its target pages.","suggestedFix":"Derive the entry UID from data that actually exists in pageProps — e.g. `(pageProps as { content?: { page?: [IContentstackPage] } }).content?.page?.[0]?.uid` — or add `entryUid` to the props returned by the slug route's getStaticProps, so the guard passes for Homepage and Discover Article pages."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":95,"description":"The useEffect registering ContentstackLivePreview.onEntryChange depends on [pageProps, routerLocale], so the callback is re-registered on every navigation and props change. If the util accumulates (rather than replaces) subscribers, each entry change will fire N stale callbacks — duplicate re-fetches racing each other — which jeopardises the criterion that successive draft changes render in sequence. If it replaces, earlier closures capturing stale pageProps are fine only by accident.","suggestedFix":"Register onEntryChange once (empty dependency array, alongside the init effect) and read the latest contentTypeUid/entryUid/locale inside the callback via a ref, so exactly one fetch is issued per entry change."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/services/contentstack.ts","line":86,"description":"`host: CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW_HOST` defaults to an empty string when the env var is unset. The SDK's built-in default live_preview host is 'api.contentstack.io' (visible in the SDK's default config), but passing an explicit empty string overrides that default, so in any environment missing CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW_HOST the preview re-fetch targets an invalid host and fails — a silent regression-by-config. The plan prescribed only enable + token; the host was added without a fallback.","suggestedFix":"Default to the SDK's host: `host: CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW_HOST || 'api.contentstack.io'` (via a named constant), or omit `host` entirely when the env var is unset so the SDK default applies."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":61,"description":"`getContentByKey<[IContentstackPage] | undefined>('page', undefined);` discards its return value and subscribes to nothing — useContent's useContext already makes the component a context consumer, so the call is a no-op whose comment ('Subscribe to the reactive page entry') misdescribes it. It is also an unused expression likely to trip @typescript-eslint/no-unused-expressions, and the surrounding LivePreviewPage wrapper (~25 lines) currently exists only to re-render children with unchanged pageProps (it cannot propagate the draft past the page-level provider — see blocker 1).","suggestedFix":"Remove the no-op getContentByKey call; once blocker 1 is fixed by making the page-level provider reactive, the entire LivePreviewPage wrapper is unnecessary — restore the original `<Component {...pageProps} key={path} />` for a smaller diff."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":34,"description":"`IContentstackPageProps` declares a `page?: [IContentstackPage]` field that is never read anywhere in the file; only contentTypeUid and entryUid are destructured. Dead interface surface.","suggestedFix":"Drop the unused `page` field (and the IContentstackPage import if it becomes unused) from IContentstackPageProps."}],"summary":"The change follows the plan's general shape — live_preview block added to the Stack options (the SDK's runtime default config confirms `enable` and `host` are real keys, and `management_token` matches the delivery SDK's documented live_preview shape, so the deviation from the plan's `preview_token` wording appears justified), live-preview-utils is initialised in _app.tsx with an onEntryChange re-fetch that correctly reuses the ContentstackFactory.createContentTypeEntryQuery pattern, and secrets are env references only (no pasted credentials in the diff). However, the feature as implemented is functionally inert on its target pages for two independent reasons: (1) the new app-level ContentstackContext.Provider is shadowed by the existing per-page providers in pages/[[...slug]].tsx:39 and pages/404.tsx:31, which are the providers every Homepage/Discover Article consumer actually reads — setPage updates a context nothing renders from, so no verification criterion requiring draft content on screen can pass; (2) the re-fetch guard requires pageProps.entryUid, which no getStaticProps supplies, so the fetch never fires. Both are blockers. Additional concerns: onEntryChange re-registration on every props change risks duplicate racing fetches, the empty-string live_preview host overrides the SDK's 'api.contentstack.io' default, and the LivePreviewPage wrapper contains a no-op 'subscription' call that should be removed. Test-file observations are out of scope per the authorship rule. Verdict: changes_requested."}
